  <h1>What Is LoD (Limit of Detection)?</h1>
  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> LoD (limit of detection) is the lowest analyte concentration a test can reliably distinguish from zero — the smallest amount the assay can actually "see". It is an analytical property, distinct from the clinical cut-off.</p>

  <h2>LoD vs cut-off</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>LoD</strong> — the analytical detection floor (how little the test can detect).</li>
    <li><strong>Cut-off</strong> — the clinical decision threshold (what counts as positive).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A test's cut-off is usually set above its LoD, so a true positive is clearly above the detection floor.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>LoD determines the test's ability to catch low analyte levels — critical for early detection (e.g., low viral load). A lower LoD means higher analytical sensitivity, which is why molecular (PCR) tests detect earlier than antigen rapid tests.</p>

